3. Use `Calder` as the owner-approved adult-fiction default unless the book already has an established series voice. `default` is an alias for Original Calder.
4. Default to a rolling three-chapter unit. Initialize it with:

```bash
/Users/drive/penname/harness/book-narrator.sh VOICE BOOK_ROOT CHAPTERS
```

Examples:

```bash
/Users/drive/penname/harness/book-narrator.sh default /path/to/book 1-3
/Users/drive/penname/harness/book-narrator.sh holden /path/to/book 1-3
/Users/drive/penname/harness/book-narrator.sh jason /path/to/book 4-6 --audience adult
```

5. Read the generated `book-narrator.prompt.md` and `run.json`. Those files give exact artifact paths and voice settings.

## Loop 1 — Understand

- Read the complete available manuscript or the current book performance bible before directing any chapter.
- Build or refresh the bible with `/Users/drive/kindling-narrator-stage/scripts/build-book-performance-bible.py` when it is absent or stale.
- Build rolling contexts with `/Users/drive/kindling-narrator-stage/scripts/write-book-performance-contexts.py`.
- Read all three selected chapters together. Track entry pressure, current turn, and next consequence.
- Use the selected voice registry entry at `/Users/drive/penname/pennamecodexv3/voices/book-narrator-voices.v1.json`. A voice choice locks identity, approved master, delivery coach, and initial provider speed.

## Loop 2 — Make the words readable aloud

For every prose paragraph, ask whether a listener can understand it once through. Check referents, causal and temporal order, dialogue attribution, sentence load, breath, and whether punctuation exposes the intended thought.

Write one item per prose paragraph to the run's `clarity-review.jsonl` using `/Users/drive/penname/pennamecodexv3/schemas/narration-clarity-item.v1.schema.json`:

- `clean`: meaning lands once and no change is required.
- `punctuation-correction`: context establishes the intended meaning, but misplaced punctuation obscures its hierarchy or directs the narrator incorrectly. Adopt the word-locked correction even when it materially changes speed, emphasis, breath architecture, or the final landing; that audible change is the repair working.
- `performance-choice`: context leaves two plausible punctuation readings genuinely viable. Preserve the original, record the alternate, and A/B render both with identical voice settings for owner choice.
- `writing-change-needed`: punctuation cannot make the meaning land once. Keep the narration copy word-locked and put the smallest suggested wording repair in `writing-monroe-feedback.md`.

Preserve purposeful long cadence, fragments, humor, voice, and owner-protected wording. Do not flatten the prose into generic short sentences.

Treat punctuation as part of the performance score. Recover intended hierarchy from the paragraph, scene, three-chapter movement, and book arc. Do not protect a chain of run-on commas merely because it creates accumulation: if it flattens distinct actions, obscures attachment, or produces the wrong oral meaning, correct it. Use `performance-choice` only when context cannot decide between two valid readings. A pending performance choice blocks the final take even though both versions pass the word lock.

Validate every prepared narration copy:

```bash
/Users/drive/penname/harness/book-narrator.sh validate /absolute/path/to/run.json
```

Compile meaning-level feedback with:

```bash
/Users/drive/penname/harness/book-narrator.sh feedback /absolute/path/to/run.json
```

Compile material punctuation alternatives for owner A/B review with:

```bash
/Users/drive/penname/harness/book-narrator.sh choices /absolute/path/to/run.json
```

Writing Monroe decides the canon repair. After an accepted change, rerun the affected paragraph and both joins. Do not render a chapter while a blocking one-pass comprehension item remains.

## Loop 3 — Codex direct, pace, render, listen, improve

- Read the cleaned chapter again in its three-chapter and book-arc context.
- Create structured direction with `/Users/drive/kindling-narrator-stage/scripts/direct-tts-chapter.sh --backend codex`, passing the selected coach and chapter context. Do not use Qwen, Ollama, or the TTS engine as the performance director for production audio.
- Direct intention, subtext, status, tactical clarity, and restraint. Temporary character color must end with an explicit return to the locked base narrator.
- Compile the direction into the `Directed-Paced` score: 1,550 ms sentence landings, 2,100 ms paragraph/speaker/subject resets, 2,700 ms written scene breaks, and only a small number of 300–340 ms pre-word preparations. Keep complete dialogue-attribution units together.
- Use local Fish Audio S2 Pro with the locked Original Calder source and approved local performance anchors. Qwen and Directed-Native outputs are historical auditions only and must not enter a new production queue.
- Never time-stretch finished audio. Provider speed is only a starting request. The approved-master WPM and pause distribution are the final pace authority.
- Run text coverage, technical mastering, measured pacing, identity/stability, and naturalness checks that apply to the renderer.
- Listen to the complete chapter. Record exact pickup locations and one cause each. Preserve accepted takes, rerender only failed passages, and recheck the joins.
- Repeat until the human listening review has no open pickup and there is no unresolved writing-level clarity problem.

Use `/Users/drive/penname/harness/book-narrator.sh status /absolute/path/to/run.json` to summarize progress. Label production artifacts `Monroe 1.2.5-CI · Directed-Paced · Fish S2 Pro · Original Calder` and add `Finished` only after the exact audio hash passes the complete owner listen.

